r pass through. Hmm, this is a real concern but we'll find out on the VM.\n\n21. **Encoding CP1251 source with Russian literals compiled on Linux**: This is a BIG one. Sources are CP1251. GCC on Linux assumes UTF-8 by default (`-finput-charset` default UTF-8, `-fexec-charset` default UTF-8). CP1251 bytes for Cyrillic (0xC0-0xFF range in the 0x80-0xFF region) are INVALID UTF-8. GCC will emit a warning `converting to execution character set: Invalid byte sequence` — actually GCC may error or warn \"converting to execution character set\" — for string literals, GCC tries to convert from input charset to execution charset. If input charset is UTF-8 (default) and bytes invalid, GCC emits `warning: converting to execution character set: Invalid byte sequence in conversion input` or actually an error? Let me recall. GCC: if the input file contains invalid UTF-8 in a string literal, gcc typically warns/errors. For C++ I believe it's an error in newer GCC (\"converting to execution character set: Invalid byte sequence in conversion input\"). Hmm. Actually, in many cases GCC treats the bytes as-is and just passes through with a warning for `-Winvalid-utf8` (GCC 14 has this). In older versions it silently passes bytes through? \n\n    The key consequences:\n    - The output strings will contain the CP1251 bytes as-is (passed through), which matches the CP1251-encoded expect files! Because check.cpp compares bytes, and the expected files are CP1251 too (t2_norm is CP1251). So byte comparison could actually PASS on Linux if the bytes pass through unchanged.\n    - But compilation might fail on invalid UTF-8 if GCC decides to convert.\n\n    Actually, let me think again. GCC's behavior: it reads source as input charset (UTF-8 default). For narrow string literals, it converts from input charset to execution charset (also UTF-8 default). When the input has invalid UTF-8, GCC historically just copied the bytes for narrow strings (no conversion when input==exec charset). Newer GCC (1