o into an ASan exe is fine. But `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`... fine.\n\n32. **t1 C uses `cos`, `sin`, `pow`, `sqrt`, `M_PI`** — needs `-lm`? On glibc, `-lm` is required for math functions in newer versions! Since glibc 2.35ish, the math functions are in libc itself? No — `pow`, `cos` etc. are in libm, and linking requires `-lm` (though GCC may link libm implicitly? No, GCC does not implicitly link libm. Actually since GCC 10, the driver links libm? No — GCC links libm for C++? Let me recall: g++ does NOT automatically link -lm. gcc doesn't either. Programs using math functions need explicit `-lm`.) \n\n    Hmm wait — on Linux with glibc, is `-lm` needed? Yes, historically yes. But newer glibc (2.35+, i.e., Ubuntu 22.04+) moved `pow`, `sqrt`, `cos`, `sin` etc. into libc proper (libm.a merged? Actually glibc 2.35 introduced `libm.so` with the functions, but the linker still needs -lm unless the symbol resolves in libc). Actually: glibc 2.35+ has been consolidating libm into libc. As of glibc 2.35, `pow`, `sin`, `cos`, `sqrt`, etc. were moved to libc so they resolve WITHOUT -lm. `sqrt` for long double etc. may still need libm. But for double, glibc 2.35 provides them in libc.\n\n    Ubuntu 22.04 = glibc 2.35, Ubuntu 24.04 = glibc 2.39. So `sin`, `cos`, `pow`, `sqrt` for double resolve from libc without -lm. Good — but this is fragile/version-dependent knowledge. The Makefile doesn't add `-lm` on Linux (LINK_FLAGS has no -lm). If the VM is Ubuntu 22.04+, it works. If it were an older distro it'd fail. We should note this as a check. Actually let me verify: glibc 2.35 NEWS: \"The functions pow, exp, log, sqrt, cos, sin, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2, ... have been moved from libm to libc\". Yes. So Ubuntu 22.04+ (glibc 2.35+) works without -lm. Good but worth noting.\n\n33. **t2 C `strtol`, `ctype`** — fine, no -lm.\n\n34. **t4 C uses `sqrt`, `isfinite`, `strtod`** — `isfinite` is in math.h as macro, fine. `sqrt` from libc on 22.04+. OK.\n\n35. **t3 C uses `sqrt`** — same.\n\n36. **run_case.cpp includes `<print>`** — needs GCC 14.\n\n37. **`check.cpp` uses `std::expected`, `std::erase`** — needs GCC 12+/14. `std::erase` (C++20) GCC 11+. OK with GCC 14.\n\n38. **t2_passport_cpp uses `std::array words{...}`** — `std::array` CTAD from initializer list of `const char*`? `std::array words{\"вершина\",...}` — CTAD deduces `array<const char*, 3>`? Actually `std::array` CTAD works: `std::array a{1,2,3}` → `array<int,3>`. Yes C++17 CTAD. GCC fine.\n\n39. **Locale / setlocale in t2 C ref.c**: `setlocale(LC_ALL, \"Russian_Russia.1251\")` — that's a Windows-specific locale string. On Linux it returns NULL (no such locale) — but ref.c isn't built by make (gitignored). t2 C main.c has `#include <locale.h>` but doesn't call setlocale (journal said removed). Fine.\n\n40. **Output formatting of numbers on Linux**: printf/print use C locale by default → decimal point '.' — good, matches expected. On Windows they don't call setlocale so '.' too. Good.\n\nSo the main blockers on the VM:\n\n**A. Toolchain prerequisites on the VM (before make works):**\n1. `g++` version ≥ 14 (for `<print>`), `gcc` ≥ 12-ish. Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 default g++ is 11/13 → need g++-14 (Ubuntu has gcc-14 in Ubuntu 24.04 universe? Actually `gcc-14` package exists in Ubuntu 24.04 (noble) universe? Hmm. Ubuntu 24.04 ships GCC 13 as default, but GCC 14 is available in the `toolchain` test PPA or in Ubuntu 24.10+. Let me not be too specific; the plan will include a check and options: `apt install g++-14` (if available) or PPA `ubuntu-toolchain-r/test`.)\n2. `make` version — 4.3+ fine (no grouped targets on Linux branch).\n3. `clang-format` installed (for format-check) — `apt install clang-format`.\n4. git, git clone.\n\n**B. Code-level Linux breakers (deterministic, will fail on compile):**\n1. Backslash `#include \"..\\common\\...\"` in t2/t3/t4 (C and C++) — must become forward slashes. t1 already uses forward slashes. This is the biggest deterministic breaker. gen_cloud/main.cpp too.\n2. `M_PI`/`_USE_MATH_DEFINES` in t1 C main.c — glibc with `-std=c17` won't define M_PI. Need `#ifndef M_PI` fallback or use `acos(-1)` or `#define _GNU_SOURCE` or `-std=gnu17`. Best portable: define fallback `#ifndef M_PI #define M_PI acos(-1) #endif` or use a constant. Also ref.c (gitignored, not built).\n3. `strcpy_s` and `strtok_s` in geometry.c — MSVC-only. glibc has `strtok_r` (signature `char *strtok_r(char *str, const char *delim, char **saveptr)` — same as MSVC strtok_s). And `strcpy_s(dest, size, src)` → need portable `strcpy`/`memcpy` or `#ifdef _WIN32` branches. This is a deterministic breaker for the whole C side (t1 C, t3 C, t4 C all use parse_point from geometry.c).\n\n**C. Uncertain / to probe on VM:**\n1. CP1251 sources with Russian literals under gcc UTF-8 default — compile warning/error? and whether output bytes match CP1251 expect files. (check.cpp compares raw bytes; expect files are CP1251 for t2/t3, ASCII/UTF8 for t1/t4.) If gcc passes bytes through, comparison still passes since both sides are CP1251. But compilation may fail with invalid-UTF-8 errors. Probe needed.\n2. clang-format version differences → format-check may flag formatting.\n3. `-lm` (glibc 2.35+ probably fine).\n4. LD_LIBRARY_PATH vs DT_NEEDED relative path (known — run from root).\n5. `.ok` dependency on `$(RUN_CASE)` and `$(CHECK)` tools — need them built first; make handles via prereqs.\n\n**D. The `make test` runs format-check first** — if clang-format on VM differs, `make test` fails even before compile. Might want a `CONFIG`-like override or `make test` without format-check, but better to just fix formatting or install matching clang-format.\n\nWait, also there's a subtle Makefile issue: the pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.c` for C — on Linux OBJ_EXT=.o. Target `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_c/main.o`. But there's also the explicit rule for `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_c$(OBJ_EXT)`. Good.\n\nAnother potential Makefile issue: `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp | $(CPP_DIRS)`. The order-only prereq `$(CPP_DIRS)` are directory targets with rule `$(CPP_DIRS) $(C_DIRS) ... : $(MKDIR) $(call WCMD,$@)`. On Linux WCMD=$1 (identity). mkdir -p build/debug/... Good.\n\nHmm, one more: on Linux, `$(call WCMD,$@)` for directories — `$@` is `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp`, mkdir -p works. Good.\n\nAlso `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case` — the directory `$(BINDIR)/tools` is in the order-only dir target list. But the recipe for tools uses `$(EXE_OUT)$@` = `-obuild/debug/tools/run_case`... wait EXE_OUT := -o on Linux, so `$(EXE_OUT)$@` = `-obuild/debug/tools/run_case`? No — `$(EXE_OUT)$@` concatenates `-o` and `build/debug/tools/run_case` = `-obuild/debug/tools/run_case`. That's valid: `-obuild/debug/tools/run_case` (no space) is accepted by GCC. On Windows `$(EXE_OUT)$@` = `/Fe:build/debug/tools/run_case.exe`? Wait EXE_OUT := /Fe: on Windows, so `/Fe:build/...` — MSVC accepts forward slashes in /Fe:? Actually MSVC generally accepts `/Fe:path` with forward slashes. Hmm, but on Windows the tools rule uses `$(OBJ_OUT)$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case$(OBJ_EXT) $(EXE_OUT)$@` two-step. Fine.\n\nWait, actually on Windows, is `$(CXX) /Fo:build/debug/tools/run_case.obj /Fe:build/debug/tools/run_case.exe ...` — forward slashes in MSVC options. MSVC does accept forward slash in paths generally? Actually `cl /Fe:build/debug/foo.exe` — MSVC accepts `/Fe:path` and forward slashes within the path are typically OK (MSVC treats both / and \\ as separators in paths). Probably fine since it already worked on Windows.\n\nOK. Also check: on Linux, `$(CXX) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<` for run_case: `g++ -obuild/debug/tools/run_case -std=c++23 -Wall -Wextra -O0 -g -fsanitize=address tests/run_case.cpp`. Good. But CXXFLAGS includes `-fsanitize=address` and the link needs ASan runtime — since it's one command (compile+link), ASan is linked. Good. But wait — no `-fPIC` needed for exe. Fine.\n\nNow, the plan. The user said: \"тестирую makefile на виртуалке Ubuntu (уже установлена)\". So the current stage is: makefile cross-platform done (mostly), now test on Ubuntu VM. This is exactly layer 15 verification (\"тот же Makefile с заполненным GCC-блоком собрал бы проект на Linux\").\n\nThe plan should be:\n1. Preparation on Windows (fix deterministic breakers) OR test-first-then-fix? The AGENTS.md methodology: reproduce, understand, fix. But some breakers are deterministic (backslash includes, strcpy_s/strtok_s, M_PI). The honest engineering flow: we KNOW these will break. But the point of the VM test is to verify the Makefile's Linux branch, and to discover things we don't know. I think the right approach for the plan:\n\n   Phase 0: On VM, check toolchain versions first (`g++ --version`, `make --version`, `clang-format --version`). This tells us if GCC 14 is present. If not, install it (this is the key prerequisite). Log versions.\n\n   Phase 1: First run `make all` on a clean clone to see what happens — this is the \"test to discover\" step. It will likely fail at compile on geometry.c (strcpy_s) and backslash includes. That's the discovery.\n\n   Actually, the user already knows from the journal that these are risks (strcpy_s flagged; backslash includes were noted as Windows-only; M_PI). Hmm, but were they? The journal 07-16-18: \"deepseek сказал, что strcpy_s - привязан к MSVC-специфичный. но раз у меня сборка под Windows, то мне не кажется что в этом есть проблема\". So it was a known-ignored MSVC-specific call. Now it matters.\n\n   I think the best plan:\n   - Phase A (VM prep): check/install toolchain (g++-14, make, clang-format, git), clone repo.\n   - Phase B (discovery run): `make all` and `make test` as-is, capture the first real failures. Don't pre-fix — the point is to see what the Makefile and code do on Linux. But maybe smarter to pre-fix the three deterministic code breakers so the Makefile testing is meaningful? The task is \"тестирую makefile\" — testing the Makefile. The Makefile's Linux branch correctness (flags, paths, .so, tools) is the focus. If source-level MSVC-isms break compile, we can't see the Makefile behavior behind them. So fixing the deterministic source portability issues FIRST is reasonable — they're code bugs, not Makefile issues. But the methodology says: don't just fix blindly; the plan can include \"fix the three known MSVC-isms in sources, since they are cross-platform defects independent of the Makefile\".\n\n   Hmm, but the plan is read-only right now (plan mode). I present the plan; the user decides. Let me structure:\n\n   Plan:\n   1. VM environment check (versions) + install prerequisites if needed (g++-14, clang-format). Verify `g++-14 --version` shows ≥14; check make ≥4.3.\n   2. Clone repo, confirm LF line endings (`file` or `git show`), run from repo root.\n   3. Pre-fix three deterministic source-level Linux breakers (these are code portability bugs, discovered by reading, not by guessing):\n      a. Backslash includes → forward slashes in t2/t3/t4 C/C++ mains (+ gen_cloud).\n      b. `strcpy_s`/`strtok_s` in geometry.c → portable: `strcpy` + `strtok_r` with `#ifdef _WIN32` to keep MSVC behavior (or just portable replacements that work on both).\n      c. `M_PI` in t1 C main.c → fallback `#ifndef M_PI` definition or use `acos(-1)` constant; keep `_USE_MATH_DEFINES` for MSVC.\n      These are safe, buildable on both. But careful: AGENTS.md says don't over-proactively change; but the task is cross-platform verification, so it's in scope.\n   4. Run `make all` on the VM. Handle any remaining failures (probe-based).\n   5. Probe the CP1251-encoding question explicitly (compile a tiny C++ file with a CP1251 Cyrillic literal; check bytes of output vs expect files) — this decides whether Russian-literal tests can pass byte-wise on Linux or whether we need `-finput-charset=CP1251`/`-fexec-charset=CP1251` or UTF-8 conversion (which is a bigger topic, deferred per AGENTS to localization layer). \n   6. Run `make test` on the VM. Watch format-check (clang-format version) and the .so /