          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "So the repo stores files as LF (`i/lf`), and with `core.autocrlf=true` the working tree on Windows is CRLF (`w/crlf`). When cloned on Linux, core.autocrlf defaults to false (on Linux), so files will be checked out as LF. Good — that means the Linux clone will have LF line endings, which is fine for make/gcc.\n\nWait, but the AGENTS.md note said \"если в будущем сделать git merge, то LF заменятся CRLF\" — that's the Windows working tree conversion.\n\nSo on the VM clone: files will be LF. Good. Makefile will be LF on the VM. That solves the CRLF problem for make.\n\nNow the real issues for Linux build. Let me enumerate the cross-platform breakage I found by reading:\n\n**1. `strtok_s` and `strcpy_s` in `common/geometry.c`** — MSVC-specific! glibc has `strtok_r`, `strcpy` but NOT `strtok_s`/`strcpy_s` (Annex K functions not implemented in glibc). This is a **compile blocker** on Linux for the C side (t3, t4 use parse_point from geometry.c; t1 C also links geometry_c.o). The journal even noted: \"deepseek сказал, что strcpy_s - привязан к MSVC-специфичный. но раз у меня сборка под Windows, то мне не кажется что в этом есть проблема\". Now it becomes a problem on the VM.\n\nFix: `#ifdef _WIN32` branches, or use portable `strtok_r`/`strcpy`. `strtok_s` on MSVC has signature `strtok_s(char*, const char*, char**)`, which is the same as POSIX `strtok_r(char*, const char*, char**)`. So a portable approach: `#ifdef _WIN32` use `strtok_s`, else `strtok_r`. And `strcpy_s(copy, len, str)` → `memcpy(copy, str, strlen(str)+1)` or `strcpy(copy, str)`.\n\n**2. `M_PI` and `_USE_MATH_DEFINES` in t1 C main.c and ref.c** — `_USE_MATH_DEFINES` is MSVC-only. On Linux/glibc, `M_PI` is defined in `<math.h>` only if `_GNU_SOURCE` or `_DEFAULT_SOURCE`... actually with `-std=c17` (strict ANSI mode), glibc does NOT define `M_PI` by default (it's a POSIX/GNU extension, guarded by `__USE_MISC` / `__USE_XOPEN`). With `-std=c17`, GCC defines `__STRICT_ANSI__`, and glibc's math.h hides M_PI unless `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` or `_GNU_SOURCE` is defined. So t1 C will fail to compile on Linux with `-std=c17` because M_PI is undefined. \n\nFix options: `#ifndef M_PI #define M_PI 3.14159265358979323846 #endif` or use `-std=gnu17` or define `_GNU_SOURCE`. Actually with glibc, `M_PI` requires `_DEFAULT_SOURCE`/`_GNU_SOURCE`. Simplest portable fix: define fallback `#ifndef M_PI` after includes.\n\nWait, but ref.c files are gitignored and not built by make (they're not in the Makefile targets — ref.c/ref.cpp are gitignored!). The journal said ref files are gitignored and used only for manual comparison. Actually, .gitignore lists ref.c/ref.cpp. But the Makefile doesn't build ref at all — tests use tests/expect files now. So ref.c M_PI issue is moot for the build.\n\nt1 C main.c uses M_PI — this IS built. So t1 C will break on Linux.\n\n**3. Backslash include paths** — t2/t3/t4 C and C++ mains use `#include \"..\\common\\exit_codes.h\"` with backslashes! On Linux, GCC treats `\\` in `#include \"..\\common\\exit_codes.h\"` as a literal backslash in the filename — the file `..\\common\\exit_codes.h` doesn't exist (backslash is a valid filename char on Linux, not a separator). So **t2, t3, t4 C and C++ will fail to compile on Linux** because of backslash include paths! t1 uses forward slashes correctly (`../common/...`).\n\nThis is a big one. The journal mentioned: \"в string_utils.h - не было прописано #include \"geometry.h\"\" but didn't catch backslashes. Actually, looking: t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp uses `../common/exit_codes.hpp` (forward slashes — good). t1_dist_matrix_c/main.c uses `../common/exit_codes.h` (forward — good). But t2_passport_cpp/main.cpp, t2_passport_c/main.c, t3_bbox_cpp/main.cpp, t3_bbox_c/main.c, t4_filter_cpp/main.cpp, t4_filter_c/main.c all use `..\\common\\...` (backslashes). And gen_cloud/main.cpp too. These will all fail to compile on Linux.\n\n**4. GCC version / std::print**: `#include <print>` and `std::print` require GCC 14+. Ubuntu 24.04 ships GCC 13. Ubuntu 22.04 ships GCC 11. So the VM's default g++ will NOT compile the C++ side (`-std=c++23`, `<print>`, `<expected>` needs GCC 12+). The journal already flagged: \"На виртуалке стоит проверить g++ --version, не гадать.\" This is a hard blocker unless a newer GCC is installed (e.g., `g++-14` via apt, or Ubuntu 24.10+). This is critical to check.\n\n**5. ASan**: `-fsanitize=address` on Linux needs libasan; Ubuntu has it. Fine. But note: running with ASan under `std::system` (run_case) — the WEXITSTATUS handling is already added. Good.\n\n**6. `$(TOUCH)` on Linux = `touch`**. In recipes: `... && $(TOUCH) $@`. Fine.\n\n**7. `$(MKDIR)` = `mkdir -p`**. Fine.\n\n**8. `.so` linking and DT_NEEDED** — known limitation, journal already documented. The `libcommon_cpp.so` is linked without `-soname`/`-rpath`. When linking t3/t4 exes against `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` by full path, DT_NEEDED records the path as given. If we link with relative path `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`, DT_NEEDED = that relative path, resolved relative to CWD at runtime. So it works only from repo root. The Makefile tests use `SET_DLL_PATH := export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=...` but for a DT_NEEDED with a slash, the loader uses it directly (relative to CWD), NOT LD_LIBRARY_PATH. Journal already knows this: \"работает только из корня\". Since make is run from root, tests should work if run from root. OK, acceptable known limitation.\n\n**9. `run_case.cpp` builds `$(CXX) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<` on Linux** — one-step compile+link. CXXFLAGS includes `-fsanitize=address` so link gets ASan too. Fine. But note: no `-fPIC` for tools — not needed for exe.\n\n**10. `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` pattern rule with `COMMON_FLAG`**: For t1-t2, COMMON_FLAG = `$(DEFINE)COMMON_STATIC` = `-DCOMMON_STATIC`. On Linux this is harmless (COMMON_API empty under !_WIN32). For t3-t4 COMMON_FLAG empty. Fine.\n\n**11. Order of pattern rules**: `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` and `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.c` — both with same target pattern. On Linux the warning journal mentioned was Windows-specific (`$(BINDIR)\\\\%.obj`). Now they use `$(BINDIR)/%` forward slashes on both? On Windows, `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT)` with forward slashes — the earlier journal (08-12) noted forward slashes work with `/Fo:`. Now pattern is `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` — forward slash everywhere. Good.\n\n**12. The `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT)` rule on Linux**: In the else branch, `CPP_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT) ...` and the .so is built from `$(CPP_OBJ)`. These are compiled with `-DCOMMON_STATIC` + PIC_FLAGS(-fPIC). Good — on Linux, symbols don't need dllexport; default visibility exports all. And since the .o are compiled with -fPIC, linking into .so is fine. Good.\n\n**13. `common/geometry.h`/`.hpp` guard**: `#ifdef _WIN32` wraps `__declspec`. On Linux COMMON_API empty. Good.\n\n**14. `setvbuf` in t4 C** — fine, portable.\n\n**15. `fflush`** — t4 C uses setvbuf but journal mentioned fflush before return. Fine.\n\n**16. Encoding**: Sources with Russian literals are CP1251 (on Windows). On Linux, gcc/g++ assumes UTF-8 by default. CP1251 bytes for Cyrillic (0xC0-0xFF range) are INVALID UTF-8! GCC may error: \"converting to execution character set: Invalid byte sequence in conversion input\" — for string literals. Actually for narrow string literals, GCC converts from input charset (UTF-8 default) to execution charset (UTF-8). If input has invalid UTF-8 bytes, GCC emits a warning/error. For GCC, invalid bytes in string literals usually produce an error: \"error: converting to execution character set: Invalid byte sequence in conversion input\". Hmm, actually I think GCC just copies the bytes through with a warning `-Winvalid-utf8` (newer GCC). Let me think...\n\nActually for C/C++ narrow string literals, GCC historically copies raw bytes. In GCC 12+, there's a `-Winvalid-utf8` warning (for C, GCC 14) that may become error. But more importantly: **the test expectations (tests/expect/t2_norm etc.) are CP1251**. If the Linux binary outputs CP1251 bytes (copied through), then `check --equal` compares bytes after removing `\\r`. Since both output and expected would be CP1251, comparison could still pass IF the bytes pass through unchanged. But there's a subtlety: GCC may validate/reject invalid UTF-8 in source. Actually for narrow literals, GCC does NOT do charset conversion if input==execution charset and just copies bytes. Wait — GCC's default behavior: input charset UTF-8, execution charset UTF-8. It copies narrow string literal bytes verbatim (only wide literals / UCNs get converted). So CP1251 bytes in literals are copied verbatim. So output stays CP1251. OK.\n\nBUT: the Makefile's `format-check` and the fact that clang-format on Linux might reformat... not relevant to build.\n\nAlso, there's a real concern: `tests/expect/t2_norm` is CP1251 (utf8=False). t2 C prints `printf(\"������...\")` with CP1251 bytes → matches. Good.\n\nBut WAIT — there's another issue. On Linux, when GCC reads the source file with default UTF-8 input charset and encounters CP1251 bytes that happen to form a valid UTF-8 sequence by accident — extremely unlikely for Russian. But invalid UTF-8 in a *comment* is fine (GCC just warns). In a *string literal* it copies. In a *character constant* it may error. Should be mostly fine. But GCC 14 added `-Winvalid-utf8` which warns. With `-Wall -Wextra` — is -Winvalid-utf8 part of -Wall? It's a separate warning enabled by -Wall in GCC 14? Actually `-Winvalid-utf8` is enabled by `-Wall` for C in GCC 14. It's a warning, not error. So compiles with warnings. The Makefile doesn't use -Werror, so fine.\n\nHmm, but actually the bigger encoding issue: **The test data files and expect files were converted to LF already** (journal 08-21: tests/expect, tests/input_data CRLF->LF). And input data files — some have no LF at all? `tests/input_data/t1_test1: total_LF=0` — it's a single line without newline (\"5\"?). Fine.\n\n**17. Windows-only `setlocale` in ref.cpp** — gitignored, not built. Skip.\n\n**18. The `format`/`format-check` targets use `clang-format`** — on the VM, clang-format must be installed. And it will try to format CP1251 files — clang-format reads files as UTF-8 by default; CP1251 Cyrillic bytes might be treated as... clang-format doesn't validate encoding much, but it may corrupt or fail on invalid UTF-8? clang-format typically passes non-UTF8 bytes through as-is (it works on bytes). Actually clang-format may complain or just pass. And `--dry-run -Werror` might fail on formatting differences (version differences between Windows clang-format and VM clang-format could flag reformatting). This is a VM prerequisite: clang-format must be installed (`sudo apt install clang-format`). And versions may differ → format-check may fail. Worth noting.\n\n**19. `make test` depends on `format-check`** first. So clang-format on VM is required.\n\n**20. Missing `$(LINK_FLAGS)` for tools on Linux?** tools recipe: `$(CXX) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<` — compiles AND links in one step, with CXXFLAGS (has ASan). No explicit LINK_FLAGS needed since single invocation. Good.\n\n**21. `run_case.cpp` on Linux**: `std::system(argv[1])` returns wait status; WEXITSTATUS used. But note: the command passed contains `> $(NULLDEV)` = `/dev/null`. And paths are forward slashes via WCMD identity on Linux. Good.\n\n**22. Potential issue: `$(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main$(EXE_EXT)` on Linux = `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main`. Pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` produces `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o` from `t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.cpp`. Good. Then link: `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^` → `g++ -fsanitize=address -o build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o build/debug/common/geometry_cpp.o`. geometry_cpp.o compiled with PIC but also fine for static exe. Good.\n\nWait — but on Linux, `T1_CPP_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main.o $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp.o`. And `geometry_cpp.o` is built by the STATIC rule `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.cpp common/geometry.hpp` with `-DCOMMON_STATIC` and PIC. Good.\n\nFor t3/t4, `$(CPP_SHARED_LINK) := $(CPP_SHARED) = build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`. The .so is built from `CPP_OBJ := geometry_cpp.o string_utils_cpp.o` (the STATIC objs, PIC). Link rule: `$(CPP_SHARED): $(CPP_OBJ) | ...; $(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(DLL) $(LINK_OUT)$(CPP_SHARED) $^` → `g++ -fsanitize=address -shared -o build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so objs`. Good.\n\nThen t3 exe: `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^` where `$^` = main.o + .so. `g++ -fsanitize=address -o exe main.o build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so`. DT_NEEDED = `build/debug/common/libcommon_cpp.so` (as given). Loader: path contains slash → treated as path relative to CWD. Run from root → resolves. OK (known limitation).\n\nBUT: `SET_DLL_PATH` recipe does `export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=... && run_case ...`. The LD_LIBRARY_PATH doesn't help because DT_NEEDED has a slash. Fine as long as CWD = root. Since make runs from root, `$(CURDIR)` = root, and run_case invokes the exe with relative path — CWD inherited = root. Good.\n\n**23. `check.cpp` `--equal` normalizes CRLF**: `rm_crlf_symbols` removes all `\\r` and trailing `\\n`. On Linux output is LF, expected LF → fine.\n\nNow let me also double check: `tests/input_data/t1_test1` len=1, no LF. It's probably just \"5\". t1 C reads via fgets → gets \"5\\n\"? No — file is 1 byte \"5\" with no newline. fgets reads \"5\", lineN[0]='5'. strlen-1 = 0 → while sz>0 skip. `lineN[0] != '\\0'`. strtol → 5. Fine.\n\nOK. So the main Linux blockers identified by reading:\n\n**A. Backslash include paths in t2/t3/t4 (C and C++)** → compile failure on Linux. MUST FIX to forward slashes.\n\n**B. `strtok_s`/`strcpy_s` in geometry.c** → compile failure on Linux (glibc has strtok_r, strcpy). MUST FIX with #ifdef or portable code.\n\n**C. `M_PI` in t1 C main.c** → compile failure on Linux with `-std=c17` (M_PI hidden). MUST FIX (fallback define or use `-std=gnu17`).\n\n**D. g++ version**: `-std=c++23`, `<print>`, `<expected>` need GCC 14+ (print) / 12+ (expected). VM must have GCC 14+. Ubuntu default may be 11/13. Check `g++ --version`, possibly install `g++-14`.\n\n**E. clang-format on VM** needed for `make test` (format-check prerequisite). Install or the test target fails.\n\n**F. CP1251 sources on Linux**: GCC may warn (-Winvalid-utf8 in GCC 14 under -Wall for C) but should compile; bytes pass through. But wait — actually there's a subtle issue: with `-Wall -Wextra` on GCC 14 C, `-Winvalid-utf8`... it's enabled by -Wall? Let me not overclaim. It might just warn. Not a blocker. But there's a real question about whether GCC 14 errors on invalid UTF-8 in *string literals*. Actually I recall GCC errors: \"converting to execution character set: Invalid byte sequence in conversion input\" — hmm, that error appears when the *execution* charset differs from input and conversion fails. When both are UTF-8 and input is invalid UTF-8... I believe GCC treats invalid bytes as-is for narrow literals. The journal earlier (07-03/04) mentioned deepseek opened files as UTF-8 and said \"символы слетели\" — that's about editor encoding, not compiler. \n\nActually, I want to be careful here. There IS a known GCC behavior: for narrow string literals, GCC does not perform conversion between input and execution charset unless they differ; it copies bytes. With invalid UTF-8 it emits a warning in newer versions but copies. So compile succeeds. I'll mark this [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] and note it should be verified.\n\n**G. `-fsanitize=address` + libasan on Ubuntu**: fine, but note ASan + `std::system` in run_case: run_case itself is ASan-instrumented, and the child is ASan-instrumented. Fine.\n\n**H. The `.o` files for the tools on Linux**: `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case` target: `$(CXX) $(EXE_OUT)$@ $(CXXFLAGS) $<`. But note that `$(RUN_CASE)` = `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case$(EXE_EXT)` — EXE_EXT empty on Linux → `build/debug/tools/run_case`. And RUN_CASE_CMD = RUN_CASE on Linux (forward slashes). The recipes reference `$(RUN_CASE_CMD)`. Good.\n\n**I. Wait — one more**: `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case` has no `.o` intermediate; but pattern rule `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.cpp` requires `.o` extension. `run_case` target is explicit rule → fine, no conflict.\n\n**J. Make version**: The `&:` grouped targets are only in the Windows branch. On Linux branch they use separate rules. Good. But `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT)` on Linux — there's a conflict! On Windows, `geometry_cpp.obj` and `geometry_cpp_dll.obj` are separate. On Linux, `CPP_OBJ` uses `geometry_cpp.o` (the static one) for the .so. And t1/t2 also use `geometry_cpp.o`. Same file used both statically and in .so. That's fine (PIC). But the rule `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_cpp$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.cpp` compiles with `-DCOMMON_STATIC` + PIC. Good.\n\n**K. `format-check` wildcard `$(wildcard **/*.cpp ...)`**: GNU make `$(wildcard **/*.cpp)` — the `**` glob is not recursive in make's wildcard (it treats `*` as matching within a path component; `**` = `*` twice = still one component each?). Actually in GNU make's `$(wildcard ...)`, `**` is treated as two consecutive `*` which is effectively `*` (same as single). So `**/*.cpp` matches `.cpp` files one directory deep — which is exactly the project structure (all sources are one level deep: t1_xxx/main.cpp, common/xxx.c, tests/xxx.cpp). So wildcard works. Fine.\n\n**L. One more consideration**: `$(CPP_DIRS)` order-only prereqs for pattern rules — directories get created via the dir rule. Fine.\n\n**M. `GREP` variable unused now** — fine, harmless.\n\n**N. `CONFIG=release` on Linux**: LINK_FLAGS empty for release, CONFIG_FLAGS `-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`. No `-fsanitize=address`. Release on Linux without ASan — fine. But t3/t4 release: `SET_DLL_PATH` still works (run from root). Note: release C++ uses `-O2` with GCC 14 fine.\n\nAlso note: on Linux release, LINK_FLAGS empty — but tools (`run_case`, `check`) built with CXXFLAGS which includes CONFIG_FLAGS (`-O2 -g -DNDEBUG`) — fine.\n\n**O. Encoding of `t2` name with quotes**: The expected output uses `«звезда»` guillemets in CP1251. Fine, bytes pass through.\n\nSo the concrete plan: \n\nThe user is testing the Makefile on the Ubuntu VM, and will type commands herself, feeding output back. So my plan should be a step-by-step VM testing protocol, plus pre-fixes to apply (in the working repo, committed/pushed) so the clone on VM has the fixes. But wait — the user's current task per startup prompt is \"тестирую makefile на виртуалке Ubuntu (уже установлена)\". And we're in plan mode. So I should produce a plan for the testing session, including the fixes needed.\n\nSince I'm in plan mode (read-only), I must NOT edit files. I should present a plan.\n\nLet me structure the plan:\n\n1. **Pre-fixes on the Windows side (before VM run)** — the three deterministic Linux blockers found by reading:\n   - Backslash includes → forward slashes in t2/t3/t4 mains (C & C++), gen_cloud.\n   - `strtok_s`/`strcpy_s` in geometry.c → portable (#ifdef _WIN32 or portable equivalents).\n   - `M_PI` in t1 C main.c → fallback define.\n   Then run `make test` on Windows to confirm nothing broke (green on Windows before going to VM). Commit, push.\n\n2. **VM prerequisites** — check versions and install:\n   - `make --version` (>= 4.4 recommended)\n   - `gcc --version`, `g++ --version` (need 14+ for `<print>`; 12+ for `<expected>`)\n   - `clang-format --version` (needed for format-check prerequisite of `test`)\n   - `git clone`, checkout branch.\n\n3. **VM run protocol** — order:\n   - `git clone` → cd → `make all` (or `make -j`) → observe.\n   - Fix as-needed with diagnostics; report errors to me with exact text.\n   - `make test` → check exit code and each failure.\n\n4. **Diagnostics plan** — each failure: exact error text, whether it's a Makefile issue (paths/flags) or a source issue (portability), hypothesis → verify → fix in Windows working tree → push → VM pulls → rerun.\n\n5. **Known-accepted on Linux** (not to fix now, note in journal): .so DT_NEEDED → only works from root (matches README requirement anyway); CP1251 sources + GCC may warn on invalid UTF-8 (verify; if it errors, decide encoding strategy — but per course, encoding layer is later; possibly `-finput-charset` if needed).\n\nAlso I should present the risk list with confidence markers per the anti-sycophancy rules, and ask which fixes to apply. But plan mode — I present the plan and get approval.\n\nLet me also verify the `-Winvalid-utf8` question: GCC 14 introduced `-Winvalid-utf8` for C, enabled by -Wall. But it's a warning. With `-Wall -Wextra` on the C code (CFLAGS includes WARN). So t1 C would compile with warnings. Fine, but worth noting that warnings may appear that aren't present on Windows.\n\nActually, wait — there's a subtlety about `-std=c17` and `M_PI`: with `-std=c17`, `__STRICT_ANSI__` is defined, so glibc's math.h does NOT expose M_PI (it requires `__USE_MISC` or `__USE_XOPEN` which come from _DEFAULT_SOURCE/_GNU_SOURCE, not set in strict ANSI mode). So t1 C main.c with `#define _USE_MATH_DEFINES` (MSVC-only, ignored on Linux) will fail: `'M_PI' undeclared`. Confirmed as blocker.\n\nAlso `strtok_s`: glibc does not implement Annex K `strtok_s`. Confirm: no `strtok_s` in glibc. Correct — blocker.\n\n`strcpy_s`: glibc does not have it. Blocker.\n\nNow also check: does t4 C use `sqrt` (math.h) → needs `-lm` on Linux! On Linux, `sqrt`/`cos`/`sin`/`hypot` are in libm, and by default the linker does NOT link libm. MSVC has it in CRT. So linking C exes using math functions on Linux requires `-lm` in the link command. The Makefile's Linux LINK_FLAGS does not include `-lm`. **This is another blocker**: t1 C, t3 C, t4 C use math functions (cos/sin/sqrt), t4 C++ uses hypot (C++ links libm automatically? g++ links libm by default? Actually g++ does link libm automatically because libstdc++ depends on it... not exactly. gcc for C does not link -lm by default. g++ links libm? Let me recall: g++ does add -lm implicitly? No — g++ does NOT automatically add -lm. Actually g++ links libstdc++ which requires libm, but the final link of an exe using sqrt from C++ still needs -lm unless something pulls it in. In practice, `g++ prog.cpp -o prog` with `sqrt` — does it link? g++ by default adds `-lm`? Hmm. I believe g++ links libm as part of the driver defaults for C++? Let me think... The g++ driver does NOT add -lm explicitly. BUT libstdc++ is linked with `--as-needed`? Actually modern g++ links `-lm` implicitly for C++ because libstdc++ has a NEEDED on libm and --no-as-needed... This is version/libc-dependent. In practice on Ubuntu, `g++ main.cpp` with `sqrt` typically links fine WITHOUT explicit -lm because the g++ driver adds `-lm` (yes, g++ DOES add -lm to the link line automatically — the driver for C++ includes -lm by default). And gcc (C) does NOT. So C exes (t1 C, t3 C, t4 C) using math functions need `-lm` on Linux link.\n\nHmm, but wait: newer glibc (2.35+) — since glibc 2.35, many libm functions are provided in libc proper? No — they're still in libm. glibc 2.35 moved some functions like `sqrt`... no. Actually there was a change: glibc 2.35 made `-lm` optional for some functions? No, that's not right. `sqrt` etc. are in libm; linking C code that uses `sqrt` without `-lm` gives undefined reference on Linux. So **t1/t3/t4 C need `-lm` in LINK_FLAGS on Linux**. This is a real Makefile gap.\n\nWait, but t1 C only uses cos/sin (in main.c). t3 C uses sqrt. t4 C uses sqrt. t1 C ref.c uses sin — not built. So yes, need -lm.\n\nSo add `-lm` to Linux LINK_FLAGS (both debug and release), or a MATH_LIB variable.\n\nAlso, t1 C++ uses hypot — g++ links -lm automatically? For C++, the g++ driver adds -lm. Actually I'm fairly sure g++ adds -lm. Let me not overstate; I'll note that C++ is usually fine but C needs -lm. Actually, to be safe: check if g++ adds -lm. Empirically: `g++ -o t main.cpp` with std::sqrt compiles and links fine on Ubuntu without -lm because g++ driver passes -lm? Let me recall the g++ link line: `g++ ... -lstdc++ -lm -lgcc_s -lc`. Yes! g++'s default link includes `-lm` (libstdc++ needs it and it's added unconditionally). So C++ is fine. C via `gcc`/`link`(g++) — but the Makefile uses `$(LINK)` = g++ for ALL links, including C exes! Line: `LINK := g++` on Linux. So even C exes are linked with g++ → which adds -lm → math resolves. \n\nSo no -lm needed because LINK=g++ on Linux. Good catch — actually that resolves the -lm concern entirely since g++ adds -lm. But then linking C objects with g++ adds libstdc++ — harmless. OK, so -lm is NOT a blocker given LINK=g++.\n\nLet me double check the Makefile: Linux `LINK := g++`. Yes (line 61). So all linking done by g++. Good.\n\nNow, back to plan. Also consider `make test` runs `format-check` with clang-format; on the VM, if clang-format version differs, formatting might differ → format-check fails → tests fail. The files are already clang-formatted with the repo's config; clang-format is fairly stable. But CP1251 files: clang-format reads as bytes, might flag? clang-format on non-UTF8 can be iffy but usually passes through. Note as risk.\n\nAnother consideration: `clang-format` on Linux may not be installed → `make test` fails at format-check immediately. The plan should install `clang-format`.\n\nAlso `make all` on Linux compiles tools? No, tools built as part of test dependencies. `make all` builds only t1-t4 exes (and their deps). Good — so `make all` won't need clang-format.\n\nNow the encoding question for running tests on Linux: the programs output CP1251 bytes (if compiled, bytes pass through). Expected files are CP1251 (t2_norm utf8=False). check.cpp does byte comparison after stripping `\\r`. So t2 norm test should pass on Linux IF compile passes and bytes pass through. For t1/t3/t4 expect files are utf8=True (ASCII, since numbers only) — fine.\n\nBut here's a real risk: GCC with `-finput-charset=UTF-8` default — if the source has invalid UTF-8 in a string literal, GCC 14 emits an error? Let me recall precisely. GCC manual: \"-finput-charset: ... invalid byte sequences are not allowed... GCC may warn or error.\" For narrow string literals, if input charset is UTF-8 and the byte sequence is invalid UTF-8, GCC's lexer treats bytes as-is for preprocessing but for string literals it... I genuinely recall projects compiling CP1251 sources with GCC fine (bytes preserved). The `-Winvalid-utf8` in GCC 14 is a warning enabled by -Wall, emitted for invalid UTF-8 sequences. It's a warning, not error. So compile succeeds with warnings.\n\nHowever! There's an important subtlety: GCC may refuse with a hard error in some cases for character constants. But our literals are strings (char*), so likely OK.\n\nI'll mark encoding as [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ] — verify on VM; if warnings flood, we can add `-finput-charset=CP1251 -fexec-charset=CP1251` or note it.\n\nNow also: **C11 `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` and `strtok_r`**: If we fix geometry.c to use `strtok_r` (POSIX), that requires `_POSIX_C_SOURCE`/`_DEFAULT_SOURCE` — under strict `-std=c17`, `strtok_r` is NOT declared! So switching to `strtok_r` also needs `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` define or `-std=gnu17`. So the C side under `-std=c17` is problematic for both M_PI and strtok_r. Options:\n   a) Use `-std=gnu17` on Linux (keep c17 on Windows). But then the Makefile CSTD differs; the journal's goal was to keep both. Actually gnu17 is fine and common on Linux; but to keep the \"C17\" claim, define `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` at top of files, or better:\n   b) Write geometry.c without strtok: manual tokenizer using strspn/strtod with pointer arithmetic — portable, no POSIX dependency. And for M_PI, define fallback `#ifndef M_PI`.\n   c) `#ifdef _WIN32` use strtok_s/strcpy_s, else use strtok_r + `#define _GNU_SOURCE`.\n\nThe cleanest minimal fix aligned with \"no platform-specific code unless #ifdef\": b) manual parse avoids strtok entirely. But that's more code churn. Option c) is minimal: add `#ifdef _WIN32 ... #else strtok_r` and `_DEFAULT_SOURCE`. But `strtok_r` needs `_DEFAULT_SOURCE`/`_POSIX_C_SOURCE` under `-std=c17`. We could define `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` at the top of geometry.c before includes (only effective on Linux; harmless on MSVC). Actually `_DEFAULT_SOURCE` is a glibc feature-test macro; on MSVC it's ignored/unknown but harmless (it's just a macro). We could `#ifndef _WIN32 #define _DEFAULT_SOURCE 1 #endif` before includes. And use strtok_r in the else branch.\n\nAlternatively simpler: replace strcpy_s with `memcpy(copy, str, strlen(str)+1)` and strtok_s with a manual tokenizer — fully standard C17, no feature macros. Actually a hand-rolled tokenizer for \" \\t\\n\\r\" delimiters isn't hard: loop strspn/strcspn. That removes the whole MSVC/POSIX dependency and works everywhere, including Windows. Given the code is small (parse_point), this is arguably the most robust and idiomatic fix, and avoids the feature-test macro mess entirely.\n\nHmm, but the user is a student; the fix should be educational. I'll propose options and let her decide. But for the plan, I'll recommend the manual tokenizer (no strtok at all) OR strtok_r with #ifdef + _DEFAULT_SOURCE. Actually the simplest that keeps Windows code path identical: `#ifdef _WIN32` around strtok_s/strcpy_s, else strtok_r/strcpy. But strtok_r under -std=c17 needs _DEFAULT_SOURCE.\n\nLet me check what string_utils.c uses — strspn, isspace — all standard. geometry.c uses strtok_s and strcpy_s — the only offenders.\n\nOK. Let me also double-check t2 C main.c and others for `sscanf`/`strtol` — standard. Fine.\n\nNow let me also check: are there `.o` pattern conflicts for `common/geometry_c.o` on Linux? The explicit rule `$(BINDIR)/common/geometry_c$(OBJ_EXT): common/geometry.c common/geometry.h` and pattern `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT): %.c` — explicit wins. Good. And for the .so, `C_OBJ := $(BINDIR)/common/geometry_c.o $(BINDIR)/common/string_utils_c.o` on Linux — same static objs with PIC. Good.\n\nOne more: `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case` on Linux — target name has no extension; explicit rule `$(BINDIR)/tools/run_case: tests/run_case.cpp`. Also pattern `$(BINDIR)/%$(OBJ_EXT)` = `%.o` — no conflict. Good.\n\nAnother: `$(RUN_CASE)` = `build/debug/tools/run_case` (no exe ext). `RUN_CASE_CMD := $(RUN_CASE)` on Linux (forward slashes). Recipes use `$(RUN_CASE_CMD) \"$(call WCMD,$(T1_CPP_EXE) < tests/input_data/t1_abc > $(NULLDEV) 2> $(NULLDEV))\" $(DATA)`. On Linux WCMD=$1 identity, T1_CPP_EXE = `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main`. Command: `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp/main < tests/input_data/t1_abc > /dev/null 2> /dev/null`. run_case runs std::system. Good.\n\nExit code: std::system on Linux returns wait status; run_case uses WEXITSTATUS → the child's exit code. Child exits 0/64/65/66/74. Good.\n\nEdge: ASan child with exit code — ASan may print leaks; exit code still as returned. Fine.\n\nNow `$(TOUCH) $@` = `touch build/debug/test/...ok`. Fine.\n\nThe `$(MKDIR) $(call WCMD,$@)` for dirs — on Linux `mkdir -p build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_cpp`. Good.\n\nSo the deterministic blockers, in order of certainty:\n1. Backslash includes in t2/t3/t4 C/C++ mains (and gen_cloud) → WILL fail compile on Linux. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n2. strtok_s/strcpy_s in geometry.c → WILL fail compile on Linux. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n3. M_PI in t1 C main.c → WILL fail compile under -std=c17 on Linux. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] (with glibc; Ubuntu uses glibc)\n4. g++ version on VM must be ≥14 for <print>/std::print (and ≥12 for std::expected). Default Ubuntu g++ may be 11/13. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ] — needs check/install.\n5. clang-format needed on VM for `make test` (format-check is a prerequisite). [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n6. CP1251 sources on Linux: warnings possible (GCC 14 -Winvalid-utf8), likely compiles; verify output bytes still match CP1251 expect. [ПРЕДПОЛОЖЕНИЕ, СРЕДНИЙ]\n7. .so DT_NEEDED → tests only work from repo root