nlikely.\n\nAlso `$(BINDIR)/t1_dist_matrix_c/main$(EXE_EXT)` on Linux = `build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_c/main`. The link uses `$(LINK_OUT)$@ $^` → `g++ -o build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_c/main ...objs`. But wait — g++ linking a C main with geometry_c.o compiled by gcc: fine, g++ handles it.\n\nHmm, one subtle thing: On Linux, t1 C exe name `main` collides with... no, distinct dirs. Fine.\n\nAnother subtle: `$(LINK) $(LINK_FLAGS) $(LINK_OUT)$@ $^` for t1 C: `g++ -fsanitize=address -o build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_c/main build/debug/t1_dist_matrix_c/main.o build/debug/common/geometry_c.o`. ASan link with g++ → libasan. Good.\n\nNow, M_PI fix options. Cleanest: at top of t1 C main.c after `#define _USE_MATH_DEFINES`, add:\n```\n#ifndef M_PI\n#define M_PI 3.14159265358979323846\n#endif\n```\nBut that only fixes if M_PI undefined. On MSVC with _USE_MATH_DEFINES, M_PI defined → fallback skipped. On Linux, M_PI undefined → fallback. Clean, minimal. (Could also use acos(-1) but a literal is fine.) Actually there's nuance: on glibc with `-std=c17`, math.h may define M_PI if _GNU_SOURCE... no. Fallback handles all. Good.\n\nWait, actually — will `-std=c17` + gcc define `M_PI`? No. Fallback needed. Good.\n\nAlternatively change CSTD on Linux to `-std=gnu17` — then M_PI defined, strtok_r declared (gnu17 defines _DEFAULT_SOURCE). That single change fixes BOTH M_PI and enables strtok_r (if we switch strtok_s→strtok_r). Hmm — that's actually an elegant minimal approach: on Linux, use `-std=gnu17` (which is GCC's default anyway, glibc extensions on). Then:\n- M_PI available (gnu17 → _DEFAULT_SOURCE) → no fallback needed.\n- strtok_r available (POSIX via _DEFAULT_SOURCE) → replace strtok_s with `#ifdef _WIN32 ? strtok_s : strtok_r` (signatures identical!). strcpy_s → replace with strcpy (portable).\n- No _DEFAULT_SOURCE gymnastics in code.\n\nThat's minimal: CSTD Linux = `-std=gnu17`, and geometry.c uses strtok_r + strcpy with #ifdef. But then CFLAGS differ (c17 vs gnu17) — acceptable; gnu17 is still \"C17 with GNU extensions\", standard practice on Linux.\n\nBut the course says C17 strictly. gnu17 is a pragmatic Linux reality. I'll present both options (A: keep -std=c17 + manual fixes; B: -std=gnu17 + strtok_r) and let her decide, recommending... Actually for learning, keeping -std=c17 and writing fully-portable code (manual tokenizer) is more rigorous and avoids the gnu17 \"extension\" question. But strtok_r + _DEFAULT_SOURCE is fine too.\n\nI think I'll recommend: keep `-std=c17`, in geometry.c replace the two MSVC calls with portable standard-C code (`memcpy` + manual tokenization with `strspn`/`strcspn`/`strtod`), and add `#ifndef M_PI` fallback in t1 C main.c. Fully standard, no feature macros, works identically on MSVC and Linux. And forward-slash the includes.\n\nActually, manual tokenizer is a nice C-exercise and removes MSVC dependency entirely. Let me sketch it for the plan (not implement now):\n\n```c\n// parse \"x y z\" from str\nchar* end = str; \nfor (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {\n    end += strspn(end, \" \\t\\n\\r\");\n    if (*end == '\\0') { free(copy); return 1; }  // too few\n    d[i] = strtod(end, &end);\n    if (*end != '\\0' && !strchr(\" \\t\\n\\r\", *end)) { ... return 2; }\n}\nend += strspn(end, \" \\t\\n\\r\");\nif (*end != '\\0') return 1;  // too many\n```\nHmm, need care with NaN/inf handling and \"нечисловые данные\". The strtod returns 0 with end unchanged if no digits — then check `end == prev_end` → not a number → return 2. This is a standard-C rewrite.\n\nThis is more robust and educational. I'll offer it.\n\nBut wait — I should be careful about scope. The user's task is \"тестирую makefile на виртуалке\". The plan mode asks me to research and present a plan. The fixes are part of the plan. I'll present the plan with a clear list of identified blockers (with confidence markers) and the testing protocol, then ask for approval to proceed (which will involve exiting plan mode).\n\nLet me also verify: are there any other `\\`