 malloc\" leftover — actually that comment was in the old code.\n\nAlso indentation is inconsistent in her new function (no indentation inside the while body at line 61, and the function body at lines 55-56 not indented). clang-format -i would fix it, but good to note.\n\nLet me also double-check: is `strtod` with a leading whitespace-only but the token later has digits — e.g. cursor=\"  bam\" (space then bam)? After a token parse, we do `cursor = end + strspn(end,...)` so cursor never has leading spaces. Good.\n\nNow, in t3 C main, parse_point is called with `s` from get_string. get_string strips nothing (keeps spaces) but stops at '\\n'. So trailing '\\r'? On Linux input files are LF. If user input has CRLF (Windows-created file piped), get_string stops at '\\n', leaving '\\r' at end? get_string reads char by char until '\\n'. It stores all chars including a possible '\\r' before '\\n'. is_empty treats '\\r' as whitespace (isspace true). parse_point: strtod(\"1 2 3\\r\") → parses 3, end at '\\r'. strchr(\" \\t\\n\\r\", '\\r') ≠ NULL → ok. cursor skips '\\r' → '\\0'. Return 0. Good, handles CR.\n\nAlright, I'm confident in the analysis. Let me also mention the formatting and that commented-out old code must be removed before commit (§ 4.1 п.10). And note the const pointer type issue (warning under /W4).\n\nActually, let me double-check her `const char*` version compiles without warnings. `strtod(pointer, &end)` where `const char *pointer` and `const char *end`. strtod signature: `double strtod(const char *restrict nptr, char **restrict endptr)`. Passing `&end` (type `const char**`) to `char**` → incompatible-pointer-types. GCC: warning: passing argument 2 of 'strtod' from incompatible pointer type. MSVC: C4090 'function': different 'const' qualifiers. With /W4 this is a warning → build not clean → would violate AGENTS. So must use `char*` for end and pointer. This is important to flag.\n\nActually wait — could strtod modify what end points to? It writes a `char*` v